{"id": "827a233d-b40e-421a-99b5-0bb0981338d9", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is a scoped impact analysis requiring CloudTrail data on Bedrock API calls by IAM users and roles across accounts to determine what would break under an SCP denying Bedrock access—this scale of cross-account scanning is token-intensive and warrants delegation rather than a manual walkthrough.

Given the "full report" request, deep research mode fits best. I'll lay out my plan for the user, then run two parallel subagents: one digging into CloudTrail for Bedrock usage by principal, and another inventorying IAM users/roles with Bedrock permissions and related resources. I need to flag important nuances—SCPs don't touch the management account or service-linked roles, and conditions scoped to IAM users won't catch role-based Bedrock calls. I also need to remind the subagents that Bedrock InvokeModel is a data event, not a management event, so it may not even be logged unless data event logging or model invocation logging is explicitly enabled—worth checking trails and CloudWatch logging config for the 90-day window. Now I'm drafting the actual subagent prompts with the right preloaded skills for infrastructure investigation, log searching, topology discovery, and documentation verification.

"}]}

{"id": "86a9b1ae-8af1-4ac6-bb76-ed3d1605c91a", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll scope this as an impact analysis across your associated accounts. One pass will check who has actually called Bedrock, using audit logs, logs and metrics. A second will check who is able to call it, using IAM users, keys, and workloads that reference Bedrock.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_do9ZbtwvW7hwHK2uaX5qIG", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-scp-usage-analysis", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "searching-logs", "exploring-metrics", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Determine which IAM USERS (and, separately, which other principals/workloads) have actually called Amazon Bedrock APIs, across ALL AWS accounts associated with this agent space, so we can assess the blast radius of an SCP that denies bedrock:* to IAM users.",
  "context": "Customer plans to attach an SCP denying Amazon Bedrock to IAM users across their accounts and wants a full impact report of what would break. This is a read-only analysis. Do not make any mutating AWS calls. Use list_associations to enumerate all accounts, and cover every one (note any account you cannot access).",
  "resources": ["All associated AWS accounts", "Bedrock endpoints: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-data-automation (events sourced from bedrock.amazonaws.com etc.)", "All relevant regions where Bedrock events appear (at minimum us-east-1, us-west-2 plus any region with activity)"],
  "timeframe": "2026-08-26T00:00:00Z/2026-11-24T18:52:00Z (last ~90 days; use CloudTrail event history lookups and any CloudTrail Lake/CloudWatch Logs-delivered trail you find)",
  "criteria": "For each account: list every distinct principal calling Bedrock, classified by userIdentity.type (IAMUser, AssumedRole, Root, FederatedUser, AWSService, etc.), with principal ARN/name, access key ID if IAMUser, event names, call counts, first/last seen, source IPs/user agents (identify SDK, console, CLI, or a workload such as Lambda/ECS/EC2), and models/resources used when available. Highlight IAMUser callers prominently, since they are what the SCP would block. Also note AssumedRole sessions whose role is assumed BY an IAM user (sts:AssumeRole chain, sourceIdentity) and whether they would be impacted depending on how the SCP condition is written (aws:PrincipalType / aws:PrincipalArn). Check whether Bedrock model invocation logging is enabled (CloudWatch Logs / S3) and use it for invocation-level identity if so; check CloudWatch metrics in AWS/Bedrock for invocation volume. IMPORTANT caveat to state explicitly in the result: InvokeModel/Converse are Bedrock data-plane events and are NOT in CloudTrail event history by default (only logged if advanced data event selectors are configured), so say clearly what coverage you actually have per account and where the visibility gaps are. Return a structured, concise report with a per-account table of IAM user callers and a separate list of non-user principals, plus confidence and gaps."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Analyze Bedrock API callers across accounts via audit logs"}, {"id": "tooluse_SDldngDDsWJIwyYV9324Ec", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-scp-iam-inventory", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["discovering-topology", "exploring-code", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Inventory which IAM USERS across all associated AWS accounts are ABLE to call Amazon Bedrock (via attached/inline/group policies), and which workloads/resources are configured to use Bedrock with IAM user credentials, to assess the blast radius of an SCP that denies bedrock:* to IAM users.",
  "context": "Customer plans to attach an SCP denying Amazon Bedrock to IAM users across their accounts and wants a full impact report. Read-only analysis only; do NOT make mutating AWS calls. Use list_associations to enumerate every account and cover each (note inaccessible ones). A separate analysis covers actual CloudTrail usage, so focus on configuration/entitlement and workload footprint.",
  "resources": ["All associated AWS accounts", "IAM users, groups, managed/inline policies, access keys", "Lambda, ECS, EC2, SageMaker, Secrets Manager/SSM Parameter Store, Bedrock Agents/Knowledge Bases/Guardrails, and connected code repositories (if any associated)"],
  "timeframe": "Current state, plus access key last-used data",
  "criteria": "Per account: (1) IAM users whose effective permissions (user policies, group policies, attached managed policies such as AmazonBedrockFullAccess/ReadOnly, wildcard bedrock:* or *:* / AdministratorAccess) allow Bedrock actions, with user name, access key IDs, key age, last-used date/service/region (flag keys last used on bedrock services via get-access-key-last-used / credential report), console password usage, and MFA; (2) workloads that appear to use Bedrock - e.g. Lambda functions, ECS task definitions, EC2 instances, SageMaker, whose code/env/config references Bedrock and whether they run with an IAM role (NOT affected by a user-only SCP) or embedded IAM user access keys (AFFECTED); look for long-lived IAM user keys in env vars/parameters where visible and in associated code repos (repo_grep for bedrock-runtime/boto3 bedrock/InvokeModel and AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID usage); (3) existing SCPs/Organizations info if visible (organizations list-policies, which accounts are management vs member; note that SCPs do not apply to the management account and do not affect service-linked roles); (4) any cross-account assume-role trusts from IAM users that lead to Bedrock-permitted roles. Return a concise structured report: per-account list of IAM users with Bedrock entitlement (marked actively used vs dormant based on last-used), workloads at risk, and gaps in visibility."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Inventory IAM users and workloads with Bedrock access across accounts"}], "created_at": null}

